    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        I generally do <strong>not</strong> recommend ordering surface lead or asbestos testing during a quick home purchase inspection window. With homes built prior to 1978 or 1980, lead paint can easily be buried under 20 layers of modern paint, or asbestos might exist in one hidden pipe elbow while testing negative everywhere else. Surface tests frequently produce false negatives or create unnecessary panic over completely intact, encapsulated materials. Unless you're planning an immediate, uncontained renovation, intact paint and undisturbed materials pose minimal active risk. If lead or asbestos is a strict deal-breaker for your household, the simplest and safest strategy is focusing your search on homes built in <strong>1980 or later</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Lead-Based Paint (Pre-1978 Homes) & Form 22J</h2>
      <p>
        Lead was widely used in residential paints prior to its federal ban in 1978. In Washington State, sellers of homes built before 1978 are federally required to provide buyers with the EPA pamphlet <em>"Protect Your Family From Lead in Your Home"</em> and complete <strong>NWMLS Form 22J (Lead-Based Paint Disclosure)</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Form 22J gives buyers a 10-day window to conduct a lead inspection, though most buyers choose to waive it. Undisturbed, painted-over lead paint is safely encapsulated. The primary risk occurs on friction surfaces (such as old double-hung wooden window sashes or door jambs) where rubbing can produce microscopic lead dust during opening and closing.
      </p>

      <h2>Asbestos Building Materials (Pre-1980 Homes)</h2>
      <p>
        Asbestos was prized for fireproofing and insulation in Pacific Northwest homes built through the late 1970s. Common historic materials include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Popcorn Acoustic Ceilings:</strong> Textured ceiling spray used heavily from the 1950s through 1979.</li>
        <li><strong>Heating Pipe & Duct Insulation:</strong> White paper tape or corrugated wrapping around basement gravity furnace ducts.</li>
        <li><strong>Vinyl Floor Tiles & Mastic:</strong> 9-inch vintage vinyl tiles and dark backing adhesives.</li>
        <li><strong>Cement Siding Shingles:</strong> Durable exterior wall tiles common on mid-century ramblers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Remodeling Rules & Puget Sound Clean Air Agency (PSCAA)</h2>
      <p>
        Testing and professional abatement become critical when you plan to alter or demolish existing building materials. In King and Snohomish counties, the <strong>Puget Sound Clean Air Agency (PSCAA)</strong> enforces strict regulations requiring an asbestos survey by a certified inspector prior to any major renovation, wall removal, or popcorn ceiling removal.
      </p>

